NOTE: THIS IS AN UNOFFICIAL FORK
================================

This repository is a fork of SnapRAID Daemon, written and maintained by
Andrea Mazzoleni:

    https://github.com/amadvance/snapraid-daemon
    https://www.snapraid.it/

The 'master' branch is kept identical to the original project. The 'storage'
branch adds a Storage page to the web UI, to set up and maintain a SnapRAID
array, with or without a mergerfs pool, from the browser. One daemon can
handle more arrays, each with its own parity, schedule and settings, along
with more pools and with disks that are in no array. The additions are
listed in the CHANGES-FORK file.

Screenshots of the Storage page, and a lab to try it with emulated disks:

    https://github.com/paleka/snapraid-lab#screenshots

Installation of the fork
------------------------

On Debian, Ubuntu, Proxmox and other systems using apt, on x86-64, one
command installs everything:

    curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/paleka/snapraid-daemon/storage/installer.sh | sudo sh

It installs the SnapRAID engine from the releases of its author, if it is
missing or older than 15.0, then the daemon from the releases of this fork,
with mergerfs and the other tools used by the Storage page. At the end it
prints the address of the web UI and its password. Run it again to upgrade:
the configuration is kept.

To keep the web UI reachable only from the machine itself, and the Storage
page read-only, as in the original project:

    curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/paleka/snapraid-daemon/storage/installer.sh | sudo SNAPRAIDD_LOCAL=1 sh

An array already in use
-----------------------

The daemon can be added to a machine where SnapRAID is already used from the
command line, and then removed again, without anything to redo. The
installer and the daemon do not touch the array, the content and parity
files, /etc/snapraid.conf, /etc/fstab, a mergerfs pool or your scheduled
jobs. They are changed only by what you start yourself from the web UI, and
the file is saved first, as snapraid.conf.snapraidd.bak and
fstab.snapraidd.bak.

'snapraid' keeps working from the shell while the daemon runs. SnapRAID lets
one command at a time work on an array: the second one, whoever starts it,
stops with "SnapRAID is already in use" and changes nothing. A sync made
from the shell shows up in the web UI within a few minutes.

What the installer does differently when it finds an array:

* The daemon starts without a maintenance schedule and without spinning down
  the disks. It watches the array and the health of the disks, and runs a
  sync or a scrub only when you start one. To hand the maintenance over to
  it, set a schedule in the Settings page and remove your own scheduled
  jobs. Add SNAPRAIDD_AUTO=1 to the command to start with the defaults of
  the daemon instead: sync and scrub every night at 02:00, idle disks spun
  down after 15 minutes.

* The SnapRAID engine is upgraded to 15, that the daemon needs. SnapRAID 15
  does not read a content file written by SnapRAID 13 or older, like the
  ones of the packages of Debian and Ubuntu: it has to be converted by a
  sync made with SnapRAID 14. The installer does it before upgrading, with
  SnapRAID 14.10 run from a temporary directory. If the array has nothing to
  sync, only the content files are written again and the parity is not
  touched. If it has changes not synced yet, the installer stops before
  installing anything and tells what to do: a real sync is your choice.
  After the conversion the older SnapRAID still reads the array.

* A SnapRAID built from the sources in /usr/local stops the installer, with
  the two commands to run first: otherwise the shell would use one engine
  and the daemon another.

If you install the packages by hand, none of this is done: convert the
content file first, and set 'maintenance_schedule' and
'spindown_idle_minutes' in /etc/snapraidd.conf as you want them before
starting the daemon. With a content file in the old format, the Storage
page tells it.

During the maintenance the daemon runs 'snapraid touch' before the sync
('touch_zero_subseconds'), which sets the sub-second part of the timestamp
of the files that have it at zero. It is what 'snapraid status' suggests,
and it can be turned off in the Settings page.

The packages (.deb, .rpm, and the source code) are in the releases:

    https://github.com/paleka/snapraid-daemon/releases

The package is named 'snapraid-daemon-storage'. It takes the place of the
'snapraid-daemon' package of the original project, with the same program,
service and configuration file, and the two cannot be installed together.
To go back to the original, install its package: the configuration is kept.

Versions are the version of SnapRAID Daemon they are based on, followed by
the number of the release of the fork, like 2.2.s1.

To build from the source code, follow the INSTALL file.

The additions are not part of SnapRAID Daemon, and they are not reviewed or
endorsed by its author. Please report problems of the Storage page here:

    https://github.com/paleka/snapraid-daemon/issues

and not to the SnapRAID project. For SnapRAID itself, see its manual and its
support page:

    https://www.snapraid.it/manual
    https://www.snapraid.it/support

Everything that follows is the README of the original project.


SnapRAID-Daemon
===============

SnapRAID-Daemon is a specialized companion service designed to move SnapRAID
from a manual, command-line interface (CLI) workflow to an 'always-on'
background service.

Under the hood, the daemon uses the same SnapRAID CLI binary, providing the
same level of reliability.

The daemon automates critical maintenance tasks, including the scheduling of
sync and scrub operations. It further enhances array reliability by monitoring
SMART values and providing a robust notification engine for real-time health
updates. For energy efficiency, it manages automatic disk spindown during
periods of inactivity.

The daemon provides a modern REST API interface along with an integrated Web UI.
This allows you to monitor array health, trigger maintenance tasks, and
configure the daemon through a user-friendly browser interface or programmatically
via the API.
